<LegalLayout
  title="Privacy · Intervene"
  description="Privacy policy for the Intervene Limited website."
  heading="Privacy policy."
  updated="August 2026 (v4)"
  current="legal"
>
  <h2>1. Who we are</h2>
  <p>
    Intervene Limited is an independent advisory practice based in the
    United Kingdom, registered in England and Wales under company number
    17317647, with registered office at 71-75 Shelton Street, London
    WC2H 9JQ. For the purposes of UK GDPR, Intervene Limited is the
    data controller for any personal data submitted via this site or in the
    course of a confidential enquiry.
  </p>

  <h2>2. What we collect</h2>
  <p>Through the contact form, we collect:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Name</li>
    <li>Organisation</li>
    <li>Email address</li>
    <li>Role (optional)</li>
    <li>The free-text description of the situation prompting the enquiry</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    With your consent, this site uses Google Analytics (GA4) to understand
    how pages are used. Analytics runs only after you accept via the cookie
    notice. Until then, and if you decline, no analytics cookies are set and
    no analytics data is collected. When enabled, Google Analytics records
    usage data such as pages viewed, approximate location derived from a
    truncated IP address, and device and browser type. We use no advertising
    trackers and do not profile visitors for marketing. Beyond Google
    Analytics and the Google Fonts CDN, which serves typeface files, this
    site uses no third-party scripts. You can change your choice at any time
    via "Cookies" in the footer.
  </p>
  <p>
    Two things are stored in your own browser regardless of that choice,
    because the site cannot do what you have asked without them. Your accept
    or decline is stored so the notice does not ask again on every page, and
    if you use the Snapshot your answers are held in the tab so your
    progress survives a refresh. Neither is used to track you, neither is sent
    anywhere, and closing the tab discards the Snapshot answers.
  </p>
  <p>
    If you reached the Snapshot on a referral link, the code from that link is
    not stored on your device at all. It is held in the page while the page is
    open and goes when you close or reload it. It says who sent you and nothing
    about you. See <a href="#referral-links">section 5</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>3. How we use it</h2>
  <p>
    Personal data is used solely to respond to the enquiry and to determine
    whether an engagement is appropriate. It is not used for marketing, not
    shared with third parties, and not retained beyond what is necessary to
    handle the enquiry or to comply with the engagement-related
    record-keeping obligations of an active or recently concluded contract.
  </p>

  <h2>4. Third-party processors</h2>
  <p>
    The contact form is transmitted via Formspree (Formspree, Inc.), which
    acts solely as the transmission mechanism. Optional Intervention
    Readiness Index contributions and referral completion notes, both
    described in section 5, are relayed the same way. Formspree is
    established in the United States, so submitting any of these transfers
    what was entered to the United States. Formspree's
    processing is governed by its own data processing terms. No other
    third-party processor receives form submissions.
  </p>
  <p>
    The site itself is static and is hosted on GitHub Pages (GitHub, Inc.).
    Serving a page involves GitHub receiving your request, including your IP
    address, in the ordinary course of delivering the site. We do not have
    access to those server logs and we do not use them.
  </p>
  <p>
    Where you consent to analytics, Google Analytics is provided by Google
    Ireland Limited (with Google LLC in the United States) and processes
    site-usage data on our behalf under Google's data processing terms.
  </p>

  <h2>5. Intervention Readiness Snapshot</h2>
  <p>
    The Intervention Readiness Snapshot asks ten multiple-choice questions
    about a single system. It is a self-reported instrument. It does not
    assess your organisation, and it does not test anything you enter against
    evidence.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Completing the Snapshot.</strong> Your answers are held in your
    own browser tab while you work, so your progress survives a refresh. They
    do not produce the result you are shown. The Snapshot is withdrawn from
    use, and the result displayed is a fixed worked example, the same for
    every visitor, rather than a reading of what you entered. No name, email
    address, organisation, role, sector or company size is requested, and
    there is no free-text field anywhere in the questionnaire. Your answers
    reach us only if you choose to contribute them to the Intervention
    Readiness Index, described below. Where you arrived on a referral link, a
    note that a Snapshot was completed is also sent, carrying the link code
    and nothing about you. Closing the tab discards your answers.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>If you ask us to contact you or to send you your Snapshot.</strong>
    This route is not currently available. The form that offered it is no
    longer part of the page, so no name, email address, organisation or role
    can be collected through it. While the form was available it asked for a
    name and work email address, an organisation and role if the visitor
    chose to give them, and a short summary of that visitor's own result, and
    those details would have been used only to send what was asked for and to
    reply. We have not confirmed whether any details were in fact submitted
    this way. Any that were are retained under the rule below, and the rights
    in section 6 apply to them. Nobody is added to a mailing list, because we
    do not operate one. The <a href="/contact/">contact form</a> is
    unaffected and remains the way to reach us.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>If you contribute to the Intervention Readiness Index.</strong>
    That is a separate, optional action with its own control. It sends your
    ten answers and nothing else: no name, no email address, no organisation
    and no role. We use these to understand how organisations answer these
    questions and, in time, to publish aggregate findings. We will not publish
    any figure drawn from a group small enough to identify a respondent.
  </p>
  <p id="referral-links">
    <strong>If you arrived on a referral link.</strong> Someone may have sent
    you the Snapshot on a link carrying a short code. The code records which
    link brought a visitor. It records nothing about the visitor. It contains
    no name, email address or organisation, none can be derived from it, and
    the person who sent it is not told who received it or whether any
    particular person did. We remove the code from the address bar when you
    land and hold it in the page while the page is open, writing nothing to
    your device. We attach it to two things: a note that a Snapshot was
    completed on that link, carrying no other information about you, and,
    where the follow-up form described above was available and used, the
    details entered there. Attribution and identification are separate events. We come to
    know who you are only if you decide to tell us.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>What we cannot yet claim.</strong> Everything described above is
    relayed by Formspree to a single mailbox. An Index contribution therefore
    arrives alongside contact enquiries and alongside any note that a Snapshot
    was completed on a referral link, and although the contribution carries no
    identifying field, we could in principle correlate two messages sent close
    together in time. We are honest about this rather than describing the
    contribution as anonymous. A future version will separate these paths
    properly, and this notice will be updated when it does.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Retention.</strong> Snapshot enquiries are handled as ordinary
    enquiries and deleted twelve months from the close of correspondence,
    unless an active engagement requires otherwise. Index contributions carry no identifier and are
    retained for trend analysis. A note that a Snapshot was completed on a
    referral link carries a link code and nothing about the visitor, and is
    deleted twelve months after the arrangement with the person who sent that
    link ends.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Lawful basis.</strong> Where you asked us to contact you or to
    send you your result while that route was available, we rely on Article
    6(1)(f), legitimate interests. The
    interests are yours in receiving the thing you asked for, and ours in
    answering a professional enquiry and judging whether an engagement is
    appropriate. We do not rely on consent here, because we could not honour a
    withdrawal without failing to send you what you requested. We do not rely
    on contract, because any engagement would be with your organisation rather
    than with you personally. You can object to this processing at any time and
    we will stop.
  </p>
  <p>
    Recording which link brought a visitor also relies on Article 6(1)(f). The
    interest is ours: knowing whether the people who share the Snapshot are
    reaching anyone at all. It is weighed against a very limited effect on
    you, because the code describes the sender and not you, is discarded when
    you close the tab, and is never used to identify you or to follow you
    anywhere else. You can object to it at any time.
  </p>
  <p>
    Contributing to the Intervention Readiness Index is different. That relies
    on your consent, given by ticking the box on that control, and you can
    complete the Snapshot and see the worked example without contributing.
  </p>

  <h2>6. Your rights</h2>
  <p>
    Under UK GDPR you have the right to access, correct, or request deletion
    of personal data we hold about you. To exercise these rights, use the
    <a href="/contact/">contact form</a>. We respond to such requests within
    one calendar month.
  </p>

  <h2>7. Governing law</h2>
  <p>
    This policy is governed by UK GDPR and the Data Protection Act 2018. The
    supervisory authority for England and Wales is the Information
    Commissioner's Office
    (<a href="https://ico.org.uk" target="_blank" rel="noopener">ico.org.uk</a>).
  </p>
</LegalLayout>
